From 8792ae760ae6e6aaf5c2cec5cbedefe573d1728e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yekai pan <pyk@seas.upenn.edu>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 23:20:36 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 22/25] l2r-local: entry 23-n: drop stack: linked runs through
 the boxes' headers

A pending drop of the glue cost memory again with the shared stack: 23-l
puts a run of one cell below the top in a 24-byte entry, and drops that
alternate release functions (the dead children of two record types in
turn) are all runs of one. lean2rr's RtAltTreeFree (mutual types A and B
with two recursive fields each, 10^6 levels, each level deferring a small
child of the other type) peaks at 88.7 MB, native Lean at 55.1 MB: the
pending vector is about 25 MB, plus its doublings. Lean's to-do list is
linked through the freed objects and takes no memory; so did the earlier
local stack (02-b, 45.7 MB there).

New: defer_wide (C: __reussir_drop_defer_wide), for a box whose first 8
bytes are header: its 32-bit count, then a 32-bit word whose upper 16
bits are zero (a variant's tag below 2^16) or padding. Boxes deferred
that way one after the other form one linked run whatever their release
functions: each links to the box deferred before it through its count and
those 16 bits (48 bits): a 40-bit signed offset in 8-byte units and an
8-bit release index, the same function as the box's or an entry of a
per-thread table of 255 functions, emptied with the stack (02-b's
encoding). The table is a zero-initialized thread-local of its own, so a
new thread's TLS image does not grow; the linked flag of a run below the
top is the top bit of its count, so an entry stays 12 bytes on 32-bit
targets. The run's entry holds the head and its function; a pop reads
the head's link, writes count 1 and the upper bits 0 back, and runs the
head's release. A box that cannot link starts a new run: one with a nonzero
top byte (under top-byte tagging an immediate's dummy box, which the glue
never defers), one farther than 2^42 bytes or not a multiple of 8 bytes
away, or a new function with the table full. The box
is the caller's alone until its release (its count was observed to be 1,
or reached 0 for an atomic box), and its release reads neither the count
nor the bits above a tag, so the header is free while it waits. The
previous head's provenance is exposed when a box links to it, and a pop
rebuilds the pointer from its address (Miri's default mode accepts it;
-Zmiri-strict-provenance rejects it, as expected).

The order is the same: a linked run is one entry of the stack like a run
of cells, so host cells, runs and steps interleave last deferred first as
before, and depth() counts it once. defer, run, defer_step, run_step,
depth, drain, releasing, set_end_work and want_end_work are unchanged;
the stack still never writes a cell deferred with defer (a host's).
Nothing calls defer_wide yet (23-o).

Tests (drop_stack/tests.rs): alternating releases in one linked entry
with no cells, headers restored before each release, a host cell and
boxes that cannot link (8-byte offset not kept, a full table) starting
runs, the table emptied after the drain; and a random program of drops,
steps and nested runs whose events are identical whether its boxes go
through defer_wide or defer. Under Miri (stacked and tree borrows) too.
